course: everyday
lesson: 250
title: Gymnastics, trampolines, winter fun and fishing
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Gymnastics looks easy when experts do it, but they learned with a coach, warm-ups and mats; flips without a coach can end with a landing on the head and a serious neck injury.
explain: On a backyard trampoline, most injuries happen when several children jump together, so one person jumps at a time with the net and padding in place, and never when it is wet.
explain: Ice on a pond looks solid but may be thin: only skate where adults have checked it is at least 10 cm thick, or on a marked rink.
explain: Sledding is safest feet first, sitting up, on a hill away from roads, trees and ponds, and you stop when it gets dark.
explain: A boy in Ontario catches a trout, wets his hands before holding it and lets it go gently, so its protective slime stays on.
fact: Gymnastics needs a warm-up, mats and a coach who teaches the moves.
fact: Cartwheels and handstands at home are practised on grass or a mat, away from furniture.
fact: Flips and backflips are not tried without a trained coach, because landing on the head can cause a serious neck injury.
fact: On a backyard trampoline one person jumps at a time, with the net and padding in place.
fact: Most trampoline injuries happen when several people jump together.
fact: A wet trampoline is slippery, so you do not jump on it.
fact: You skate only on ice checked by adults or on a marked rink; the ice must be at least 10 cm thick.
fact: Sledding is done feet first, sitting up, on a hill away from roads, trees and ponds; head first, your head hits any obstacle first.
fact: Sledding stops when it gets dark, because you cannot see obstacles.
fact: Snow, especially yellow or dirty snow, is not for eating, because it can be dirty.
fact: A fish hook stuck in a finger is not pulled out; an adult or doctor removes it, because the barb (the little backward hook on its point) tears skin.
fact: Many places require a fishing licence, for adults and sometimes for children, to protect fish stocks (the number of fish living in the water).
fact: To release a fish, you wet your hands and put it back gently, because dry hands damage its slime coat.
exercise: What do you do before a gymnastics class? | Warm up | Eat a big meal | Do the hardest move first | Stretch nothing
exercise: Where should you practise cartwheels at home? | On grass or a mat, away from furniture | On the smooth kitchen tiles, where there is lots of room | Between the sofa and the table | On the stairs, holding the banister
exercise: You saw a backflip on video and want to try it. What is safe? | Learn it only with a trained coach | Try it on the floor at home after watching the video again | Try it on your bed | Try it on a wet trampoline, because wet ones are softer
exercise: Why are flips without a coach dangerous? | Landing on the head can injure the neck | They make you dizzy for a whole week afterwards | They tire your arms | They damage the floor
exercise: How many children should jump on a backyard trampoline at once? | One | Two | As many as fit | Three, holding hands
exercise: When do most trampoline injuries happen? | When several people jump together | When one person jumps alone in the middle | When the safety net is on | When someone jumps slowly and gently
exercise: It rained in the morning. Can you jump on the trampoline? | Not while it is wet, it is slippery | Yes, because a wet trampoline is much bouncier | Yes, if you jump higher | Yes, but only with the safety net taken off
exercise: Your friends want to skate on a frozen pond nobody has checked. What do you do? | Skate only on checked ice or a rink | Go to the middle where it is smooth | Test it by jumping on it | Go first because you are light
exercise: How thick must ice be to skate on? | At least 10 cm | At least 1 cm | Thick enough to look white | As thick as a book cover
exercise: How should you ride a sled? | Feet first, sitting up | Head first, lying down | Standing up | Backwards, lying down
exercise: Where should you go sledding? | On a hill away from roads, trees and ponds | Down a hill that ends at a road | Between the trees, where the snow is deepest and softest | Down onto a frozen pond so the sledge goes further
exercise: It is getting dark on the sledding hill. What do you do? | Stop sledding | Go faster to finish | Sled down one last time in the dark | Use a torch on the sled
exercise: Should you eat snow? | No, it can be dirty | Yes, especially yellow snow | Yes, it is like ice cream | Yes, dirty snow is cleanest
exercise: A fish hook goes into your finger. What do you do? | Leave it in and get an adult or doctor | Pull it out quickly, in one hard tug | Push it right through by yourself | Cut it out with a knife
exercise: You want to let a fish go. How do you hold it? | With wet hands, then return it gently | With dry hands, squeezing it tightly | With a towel, then throw it back | By the eyes
exercise: Why do many places require a fishing licence? | The rules protect the fish stocks | To sell more fishing rods | So children cannot fish | To make the fish bite
test: A girl in Utah does handstands in the living room next to the glass coffee table. What should she do instead? | Practise on grass or a mat away from furniture | Practise closer to the table so she can lean on it | Practise only at night | Hold on to the edge of the glass table for balance
test: Four cousins bounce on one trampoline at the same time. What is the main danger? | They can crash into each other | The trampoline gets far too bouncy with four on it | They will get tired quicker | Nothing, because the safety net stops everything
test: Why is a barbed fish hook not pulled out of a finger? | The barb tears the skin | It goes in deeper by itself | The fish might want it back | It is easier to leave it forever
test: A boy in Minnesota sees a sign on a lake saying "Ice unsafe". His friend says it looks solid. What is right? | Believe the sign and stay off | Walk on it carefully | Test it near the middle | Skate quickly so it does not break
test: Why is head-first sledding more dangerous than feet first? | Your head hits obstacles first | Your feet get cold | It is slower | You cannot steer with your feet
test: A boy in Scotland catches a fish and holds it in his dry hands for a long photo. What harm can that do? | It damages the fish's slime coat | It makes the fish grow faster | It warms the fish up nicely after the cold river | No harm at all, as long as it goes back in the end
test: Why is a sledding hill that ends at a road a bad choice? | You may slide into traffic | The snow there is too soft | The road makes the sled slower | Sleds cannot cross roads
test: Your gym club has mats, a coach and a warm-up. Why is it safer than trying moves alone? | You learn the right technique with protection | You can do any move you like there | Coaches catch you every time, so you can never fall | Mats make you jump higher
test: Snow at the side of a busy road is grey. Why not eat it? | It is dirty | It tastes like rain | It is too cold to swallow | It is made of different water
test: me n 3 mates on the trampoline at once, more fun!! | No, one person jumps at a time | Yes, more people means safer bounces | Yes, if you hold hands | Only if the net is off
test: pond's frozen, looks thick, can i skate | Only if adults have checked the ice | Yes, if it looks white | Yes, if you go slowly | Only near the middle
test: gonna try a backflip on the grass, saw it on youtube | No, only with a trained coach | Yes, grass is soft enough | Yes, if you try it twice slowly | Only on the trampoline alone
test: hook's stuck in my finger, gonna yank it out | Don't pull it, let an adult or doctor remove it | Pull it fast n hard | Twist it round and pull, that gets it out | Cut the line and leave it
explain: Three children in Colorado bounced on a trampoline at the same time; the smallest was thrown off sideways and broke her arm.
explain: A boy in Michigan went ice fishing alone on early winter ice; it cracked under him and he only got out because a man nearby heard him shout.
fact: Most trampoline injuries happen when more than one person jumps at once, and the smallest child is hurt most.
fact: Early-winter and late-winter ice can be thin, and falling through it is life-threatening within minutes.
test: 3 of us on the tramp at once, more fun | One at a time, the smallest one gets hurt | Fine if you hold hands | Fine if the net is up | Only big kids together
test: ice on the lake looks thick, can i go fish alone | No, only with an adult who has checked the ice | Yes, if it's white | Yes, if you stay quick n light on ur feet | Only near the middle, where it's thickest
test: Why are flips on a trampoline so risky? | Landing on your head or neck can cause bad injuries | They are too slow | They wear out the springs much faster than jumping | They are only risky outside
test: did u hear 4 kids were on the trampoline at once and the littlest one broke her leg? real lesson? | One person jumps at a time | Little ones should jump in the middle | More kids makes it bouncier and safer | Only jump with your friends, not siblings
test: apparently a kid got a fish hook in his finger and his dad yanked it out and it tore half his finger. what's the true lesson? | Don't pull a hook out; an adult or doctor removes it | Pull it out fast yourself so it's quicker | Push it further in to fix it | Leave it in for a week n let it fall out by itself
test: my mate says he did a backflip on the grass with no coach and he's fine, so they're easy. right? | No, flips need a trained coach; landing on the head is serious | Yes, grass makes flips safe | Yes, if you're brave | Yes, if your mate spots you
exam: Identify the minimum thickness of ice upon which one may skate. | At least ten centimetres | At least one centimetre | At least two centimetres | No minimum is required
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises most trampoline injuries? | They occur when several persons jump together | They occur only when a person jumps alone | They occur only on wet days, when the mat is slippery | They occur only to adults
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice in sledding. | Sitting up, feet first, on a hill away from roads, trees and ponds | Lying head first towards a road | Sledding down towards a frozen pond | Sledding after dark on any hill
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the hands are wetted before a fish is released. | Dry hands damage the fish's slime coat | Wet hands grip the fish more tightly | Fish prefer to be handled cold | It cleans the hands of bait
